    Let's assume:

    main
    │
    └── git-revert
            │
            └── sub_git_revert (HEAD)
                    \
                    A   ← This commit should have been on git-revert

    You accidentally committed A on sub_git_revert, but you wanted it on git-revert.

    Case 1: The commit has NOT been pushed (most common)

    Suppose A is the latest (and only accidental) commit on sub_git_revert.

    Step 1: Save the commit hash
    git log --oneline

    Example:

    abc1234 Fix login bug   <-- A
    def5678 Previous commit

    Copy abc1234.

    Step 2: Switch to the correct branch
    git switch git-revert
    Step 3: Cherry-pick the commit
    git cherry-pick abc1234

    Now your history looks like:

    main
    │
    └── git-revert
            │
            ├── A
            │
            └── sub_git_revert
                \
                    A

    The same changes now exist on both branches (with different commit hashes).

    Step 4: Remove it from sub_git_revert

    Switch back:

    git switch sub_git_revert

    Reset one commit:

    git reset --hard HEAD~1

    Final result:

    main
    │
    └── git-revert
            │
            ├── A
            │
            └── sub_git_revert

    Exactly what you wanted.

    Case 2: The commit HAS already been pushed

    If other people may have pulled the branch, avoid rewriting its history.

    Instead:

    Cherry-pick onto git-revert:
    git switch git-revert
    git cherry-pick abc1234
    Go back to sub_git_revert:
    git switch sub_git_revert
    Revert the accidental commit:
    git revert abc1234

    Now the history records both the accidental commit and the fact that it was undone, making it safe for shared branches.

    Case 3: The accidental commit is the most recent commit and no one has pulled it

    There's an even quicker method using git reset:

    On sub_git_revert:

    git reset --soft HEAD~1

    This removes the commit but leaves all its changes staged.

    Now switch branches:

    git switch git-revert

    If Git refuses because the staged changes would conflict, you can instead stash them:

    git stash
    git switch git-revert
    git stash pop

    or use the first (cherry-pick) method, which is generally simpler and safer.

    Finally:

    git commit -m "Your commit message"

# moving a commit to a new branch ( not an existing one)

---

        git branch new_branch
        git reset --hard <commit-hash>
        git checkout new_branch
        git add .
        git commit -m "commit moved  to new branch"

---
